use /lib

from /scope/harness-integration import HarnessIntegrationScope
from /scope/project-directory import ProjectDirectoryScope
from /scope/project-locations import ProjectLocationsScope
from /scope/prompt-mode/mode-system-prompts import ModeSystemPromptsScope
from /scope/run-targets import RunTargetsScope

export scope ProjectDataScope:
    concept: {ProjectDataConcept}

concept ProjectDataConcept:
    pitch:
        What the application keeps for a project, kept in the project itself so
        it survives a restart and travels with the project.

    shape: {ProjectDataShape}

shape ProjectDataShape:
    description:
        Everything the application keeps for a project lives in a `.suspense`
        directory inside the @{ProjectDirectoryScope}, created as it is needed.
        Nothing about a project is kept anywhere else, apart from the draft.

    contents:
        - `history/`: every task sent, as its hidden anchor's source, named by the second it was sent and the anchor's name, and beside it, once its run is over, a `.json` record of the same name holding the compiled userPrompt the harness received, the system prompt as it received it and the harness it went to, whether it has been marked done by hand for sending to the other mode, each line the harness printed, each message sent to the task while it ran, in its place among those lines, any error, and, in a git repository, the hashes of the two snapshots of the working tree taken as it started and ended, as the task snapshots below say
        - `snapshot-index`: in a git repository, the private index the task snapshots are built with, as they say
        - `images/`: every image attached to a task or question, saved when it is sent or queued, named by the second it was saved and a short hash of its content, with its own extension, so the same image attached twice is saved once
        - `asks/`: every question asked from the Ask tab, saved the same way as a task but apart from them
        - `queue/`: each queued prompt, as its hidden anchor's source, imports included, named so they list in the order they were queued, marked when each was queued rather than when it was saved, so prompts queued one straight after another keep their order however long each takes to save
        - `divergence/`: each divergence report, a JSON file named by when it was made
        - `system-prompts/`: each mode's system prompt template, a Markdown file named after its mode, code.md, combined.md for Chain, spec.md, and ask.md, and the spec-reading prompt injected into them, spec-reading.md
        - `commit-notes.json`: the Git panel's commit notes
        - `run.json`: the project's run targets, as the @{RunTargetsScope} says
        - `harness.json`: the last report the harness gave of the skills and agents it has
        - `fluency.md`: the project's Piton fluency, as `piton agent --print-fluency` printed it at the last spec build, generated rather than edited, for the Spec and Chain prompts to point at, as the @{ModeSystemPromptsScope} says
        - `conversations.json`: which conversation of the tasks, and of the questions, was last left for a new one, as the @{HarnessIntegrationScope} says, so it isn't carried on again once the project is reopened

        The draft, `.suspense-draft.pi`, isn't in `.suspense`: it is the prompt
        being written, as `piton lsp` is shown it, a document named as if it
        were in the spec location, as the @{ProjectLocationsScope} says, so the
        server resolves its imports.

    machineOnly:
        Some of it belongs to one machine rather than to the project: the
        harness report `.suspense/harness.json`, the fluency
        `.suspense/fluency.md`, printed by this machine's piton, the queue
        `.suspense/queue/`,
        the commit notes `.suspense/commit-notes.json`, the conversations left
        `.suspense/conversations.json`, whose conversations only this machine's
        harness knows, the snapshot index `.suspense/snapshot-index`, and the
        draft
        `.suspense-draft.pi` wherever it is, should it ever be written. Git
        ignores these, so they are never committed. Everything else, the prompt
        history, the questions, the divergence reports, and the system prompts,
        is kept with the project.

    taskSnapshots:
        In a project that is a git repository, every task the harness runs
        keeps a record of the files that changed while it ran. When the harness
        actually starts the task, not when it is sent or queued, the project's
        working tree is snapshotted, and it is snapshotted again when the run
        is over, however it ends: done, failed, or cancelled. In a project that
        isn't a git repository, nothing is snapshotted or recorded, and nothing
        about it is shown anywhere.

        A snapshot is a git tree, made with git's plumbing alone: `git add -A`
        and then `git write-tree`, run with `GIT_INDEX_FILE` pointing at a
        private index, `.suspense/snapshot-index`, so the repository's own
        index, HEAD, branches, stash, and working tree are never read from or
        written to. The private index is kept between snapshots, so each one
        hashes again only the files whose timestamps changed since the last.
        A snapshot follows `.gitignore`, as git does, so ignored files,
        `.suspense`'s machine-only data among them, are never in it. No commit,
        branch, worktree, or stash is ever made, nothing is added to the
        user's history, and no tool but git is needed.

        Both trees are pinned so git's garbage collection never deletes them,
        each under a private ref, `refs/suspense/<task>/before` and
        `refs/suspense/<task>/after`, `<task>` being the task's hidden anchor's
        name, and their hashes are kept in the task's history record. A
        snapshot that can't be taken, as when git fails, is left out rather
        than stopping the task; a task missing either snapshot shows no changed
        files.

        The private index and the refs belong to this machine: `snapshot-index`
        is ignored by git like the other machine-only data, and the refs are
        never pushed, since nothing pushes `refs/suspense/`.

    reading:
        What can't be read is left out, or shown as missing, rather than
        stopping the rest from being read.

    lastProject:
        When the application starts, it opens the project that was opened most
        recently, the first of the @{ProjectDirectoryScope}'s recent projects
        that still holds its piton.config.pi. With none, it starts with no
        project open.
